  "query_preview": "..\nHeavy rainfall and flash flooding occurred overnight and this \nmorning across Guam. This rainfall event was associated with the \npassage of Invest 95W, which remains a weak disturbance now east of \nthe Marianas. Additional scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms will be possible across Guam tonight. Drier conditions \nare seen across the CNMI, but a few showers and brief downpours will \nbe possible.\n\nAs Invest 95W exits the region, drier conditions are expected \nthrough the rest of the weekend and through much of next week. Winds \nwill be light and variable through much of next week. Model Time- \nHeights do show a shallow moisture setup through the first half of \nthe week across the Marianas. Relative Humidity values look to fall \nto 30 percent around 700mb. This should help put a lid on potential \nisland-effect days this week. Towards the middle and latter portions\nof the week, moisture will return to the region with winds forecast \nto remain light through the atmosphere on Wednesday, which could \nallow for some island-effect showers. At this time, shower coverage \nremains isolated due to uncertainty in the quality of moisture depth.\n\nLong range guidance continues to hint at a disturbance approaching \nthe region next weekend. At this time, uncertainty remains high on \nany potential solution due to no trackable disturbance and a time \nframe that is 6 to 8 days out. As we continue to monitor the \nsituation, it is important to emphasize that October is within peak \ntyphoon season in the western Pacific. Residents in the Marianas \nshould continue to monitor forecasts over the next several days, and \nmaintain a general state of preparedness.",
